=======================================================================================================================================================\
 A strong workflow has two separate prompts.\
\
  ### 1. Planning prompt\
\
  Create an implementation-ready plan for this task.\
\
  Rules:\
  - Inspect the repository before planning.\
  - Do not modify files.\
  - Do not assume missing behavior, paths, schemas, or interfaces.\
  - Ask questions for any decision that cannot be verified from the repository.\
  - The plan must be specific enough that another engineer can implement it without making design decisions.\
\
  The plan must include:\
\
  1. Exact objective.\
  2. Explicit in-scope behavior.\
  3. Explicit out-of-scope behavior.\
  4. Exact files allowed to change.\
  5. Exact files that must not change.\
  6. Existing functions/classes/interfaces to reuse.\
  7. New interfaces, schemas, fields, or CLI commands.\
  8. Input/output examples.\
  9. Error and warning behavior.\
  10. Edge cases and failure modes.\
  11. Test files and exact test scenarios.\
  12. Validation commands and acceptance criteria.\
  13. Assumptions, with no unresolved assumptions hidden.\
\
  Before finalizing, check:\
  - Can implementation proceed without guessing?\
  - Can every planned change be mapped to an allowed file?\
  - Is every acceptance criterion objectively testable?\
  - Is there any scope not explicitly approved?\
\
  ### 2. Implementation prompt\
\
  Implement only the approved plan below.\
\
  Execution rules:\
  - First read the plan and inspect the current worktree.\
  - Modify only the files listed in \'93Allowed files\'94.\
  - Do not create new files unless explicitly listed.\
  - Do not refactor, rename, reformat, clean up, or improve unrelated code.\
  - Do not change public behavior outside the stated objective.\
  - Do not add dependencies unless explicitly approved.\
  - Do not guess. If the repository contradicts the plan, stop and report:\
    1. the contradiction;\
    2. the relevant file and line;\
    3. the smallest decision needed.\
  - Preserve all pre-existing user changes.\
  - Run only the listed validation commands, plus minimal commands needed to diagnose failures.\
\
  Before editing, report:\
  - files you will change;\
  - files you will not change;\
  - expected tests.\
\
  After editing, report:\
  - exact files changed;\
  - plan item implemented for each change;\
  - tests run and results;\
  - any deviation from the plan.\
\
  Approved plan:\
  ...\
\
  ### The most important plan sections\
\
  Require these fields in every plan:\
\
  Allowed files:\
  - scripts/validate_pr_impact_step0.py\
  - tests/test_validate_pr_impact_step0.py\
  - examples/pr-impact/ia-app-pr-49156.yaml\
  - examples/pr-impact/ia-app-pr-48706.yaml\
\
  Forbidden changes:\
  - no catalog schema changes\
  - no repo-v1 extraction changes\
  - no GitHub API integration\
  - no dependency changes\
  - no unrelated documentation changes\
\
  New interface:\
  Command:\
    python scripts/validate_pr_impact_step0.py --fixture PATH --repo-root PATH\
\
  Exit codes:\
    0 = valid, warnings allowed\
    1 = validation errors\
    2 = invalid command usage\
\
  Output:\
  - human-readable summary by default\
  - stable JSON only with --json\
\
  Acceptance criteria:\
  - both golden fixtures pass\
  - malformed YAML fails\
  - missing Git revision fails\
  - unresolved coverage produces warning only\
  - no files outside the allowed list change\
\
  ### Add a \'93change budget\'94\
\
  This is especially effective:\
\
  Change budget:\
  - Maximum files changed: 4\
  - New production files: 1\
  - New test files: 1\
  - Documentation changes: 0\
  - Dependency changes: 0\
  - Database/schema changes: 0\
\
  The agent should check the budget before finishing.\
\
  ### Require a stop condition\
\
  Use this exact rule:\
\
  > If implementation requires a decision not answered by the plan or discoverable from existing code, stop. Do not choose a reasonable default. Ask for\
  > clarification.\
\
  That prevents most drift.\
\
  ### Use staged execution\
\
  For larger work, ask the agent to implement only one slice:\
\
  Implement Phase 1 only.\
  Do not prepare Phase 2.\
  Phase 1 is complete only when its tests pass and the worktree diff contains only the approved files.\
\
  Then review the diff before authorizing the next phase.\
\
  The practical formula is:\
\
  > inspect first \uc0\u8594  decide explicitly \u8594  list allowed files \u8594  define interfaces \u8594  define failure behavior \u8594  define tests \u8594  define forbidden work \u8594 \
  > implement one slice \uc0\u8594  verify diff.\
\
  That combination gets you much closer to \'93the implementation matches the plan\'94 instead of \'93the agent interpreted the plan.\'94}